narrative:
## Modeling a Sports Player

A player has a name, jersey number, and field position. A struct bundles these together so you can pass one value instead of three.

### The Player Struct

```xiom
type Player = {
  name: Str;
  number: Int;
  position: Str;
}
```

### The Team Struct

A team has a name and sport, plus you track its star player:

```xiom
type Team = {
  name: Str;
  sport: Str;
  star: Player;
}
```

### Creating Players and Teams

```xiom
let messi = Player{ name: "Lionel Messi"; number: 10; position: "Forward"; };

let fc_barcelona = Team{
  name: "FC Barcelona";
  sport: "Football";
  star: messi;
};
```

### Announcing a Player

A function that produces a stadium-style introduction:

```xiom
fn announce(p: Player) -> Str {
  return "Number " + p.number + ", playing " + p.position + " — " + p.name + "!";
}
```

Using it:

```xiom
announce(messi);  // "Number 10, playing Forward — Lionel Messi!"
```

### Accessing Nested Data

```xiom
fc_barcelona.star.name;      // "Lionel Messi"
fc_barcelona.star.number;    // 10
fc_barcelona.sport;          // "Football"
```

The dot chain reads left to right: team ? star player ? field.

